WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.834
winning actress whose new film
"She Came to Me"

00:00:04.834 --> 00:00:06.868
is in theaters tomorrow,
October 6th.

00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:07.934
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:07.934 --> 00:00:09.534
the very talented,
our dear friend

00:00:09.534 --> 00:00:11.067
Anne Hathaway, everybody.

00:00:11.067 --> 00:00:13.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:23.667
♪♪

00:00:23.667 --> 00:00:32.033
♪♪

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:34.367
Welcome back, Anne.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.667
-I'm so happy you're here,
and I want to congratulate you.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:39.434
Our lives run in parallel
in many ways.

00:00:39.434 --> 00:00:41.400
We just -- my wife
and I celebrated our 10th.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.234
You guys just celebrated
your 11th anniversary.

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:44.567
-Happy anniversary.
-Congratulations.

00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:45.767
-Congratulations to you, too.
-Thank you very much.

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:48.067
What did you guys do
for the big 11?

00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:49.901
-Got rained out.
-You got rained out?

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:51.968
Was it this...?
-Yes, it was this past Friday?

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:54.801
-Oh, yeah.
That's a terrible day.

00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:57.701
That's literally the skies
sending an omen, yeah.

00:00:57.701 --> 00:01:00.467
-That you should just
let your ambition be dead.

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:02.968
-Yeah.
-And so we did, we did.

00:01:02.968 --> 00:01:05.200
We just kind of like,
re-pajamaed

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:06.434
-Oh, that's nice.

00:01:06.434 --> 00:01:08.100
-I think if you can
re-pajama with someone

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.300
that means that
you've chosen the right mate.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.334
--You know, I had a moment

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.767
when I was curled up
on his chest,

00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:15.634
and we were watching, like, hour
three of "Abbott Elementary,"

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:17.634
and I was like,
I'm so happy.

00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:20.501
-There you go.
-This is the right guy for me.

00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:22.234
And I did have that thought that
that I --

00:01:22.234 --> 00:01:23.234
What did you guys do?

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:25.601
-We, it was September 1st

00:01:25.601 --> 00:01:27.667
and we had --we had
a lovely dinner party

00:01:27.667 --> 00:01:29.901
where we all -- it was
a five couples --

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:32.133
and we all went around
and talked about

00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:34.834
how we met, the day we met.

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:37.033
And unfortunately,
the last person,

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:41.367
the last guy who told the story
made it -- was way more romantic

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.334
than the first
four guys had been,

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:47.567
in a way that we all were like,
"Could we go again?"

00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:49.067
-Actually, I want
to change my answer.

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:50.467
-I would love to change it.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:51.701
I didn't realize you were
allowed to talk like that.

00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:54.234
-You and I met
just for the first time

00:01:54.234 --> 00:01:56.300
just before he and I met
or got together.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.834
-Really?
-Because, yeah,

00:01:57.834 --> 00:01:59.434
in October 2008,

00:01:59.434 --> 00:02:01.467
I hosted "Saturday Night Live"
for the first time.

00:02:01.467 --> 00:02:02.734
-Yeah, I remember well.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:04.834
-And then I went out to --
I remember it very well.

00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:07.400
It was one of the most
exciting weeks of my life.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.868
It still is.

00:02:08.868 --> 00:02:11.567
And then -- and then I went out
to L.A. to work on a movie,

00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:13.234
and that's when Adam
and I got closer.

00:02:13.234 --> 00:02:16.400
And then it was
the North American premiere

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:17.701
of "Rachel Getting Married,"

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:19.834
the Jonathan Demme film.
-There you go, Jonathan Demme

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:21.434
is a great director.
-So that month in my life

00:02:21.434 --> 00:02:22.834
was just crazy.

00:02:22.834 --> 00:02:23.934
-That's so fantastic.

00:02:23.934 --> 00:02:25.400
The other thing I remember,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.834
The Killers were
the musical guests.

00:02:26.834 --> 00:02:28.567
-Yes.
-And that was a --

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:30.434
that show also,
I should note,

00:02:30.434 --> 00:02:33.434
was right after
the last writers strike.

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:34.734
That was, yeah.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:36.033
I forgot about that part.
Wow.

00:02:36.033 --> 00:02:38.467
-And so it was we were
so happy to be back at "SNL"

00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:41.300
and you were such
an exceptional host,

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.934
and so that was really special.

00:02:42.934 --> 00:02:44.901
And it was just a -- it
was just a banger of a show,

00:02:44.901 --> 00:02:46.667
I remember, start to finish.
But my parents were there

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.167
and The Killers
were the musical guests.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:51.234
And my parents, I introduced
my parents to The Killers

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:52.400
who were really nice.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.234
And as they walked away,
my mom said very loud

00:02:54.234 --> 00:02:55.501
and they definitely heard,
"I don't know why

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:56.501
they call them The Killers.

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:57.667
They couldn't be nicer.

00:02:57.667 --> 00:03:00.634
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.634 --> 00:03:04.000
I'm like, "Well, did you think
they were murderers?"

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.467
-So my mom came to one
of my --

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:09.133
My little brother
did stand up for a while.

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:11.267
He's really funny,
and my mom and dad,

00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:13.334
you know,
went to one of his shows.

00:03:13.334 --> 00:03:16.400
And one of the comics
before him

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.767
explained Tinder
to the audience.

00:03:18.767 --> 00:03:19.968
And then in the pause
that followed,

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:22.000
because most people
knew what Tinder was

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.234
but my mother didn't,

00:03:23.234 --> 00:03:24.968
you just hear
my mom's voice ring out,

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:26.467
"Oh, that's awful!"

00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:30.300
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.834
-That's what you want to hear
on stage as a comic.

00:03:33.834 --> 00:03:37.300
This -- So this film, this is
a beautiful independent film.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.267
Obviously, there's
a SAG-AFTRA strike.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.734
This is a film with a waiver
that is given

00:03:41.734 --> 00:03:43.934
to some of the independent films
that are not made

00:03:43.934 --> 00:03:46.634
under sort of the auspices
of the struck companies.

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.234
Is it nice to be able
to come up

00:03:48.234 --> 00:03:50.400
and talk about sort of
one of these smaller films

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.834
that sometimes don't get
the same amount of attention?

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:55.033
-Yeah.
Yeah, it's amazing.

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:56.567
I mean, for a lot of reasons.

00:03:56.567 --> 00:04:00.501
One, Rebecca Miller is
our writer and our director,

00:04:00.501 --> 00:04:02.234
and I think she's been
working on this project

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:03.567
for close to a decade.

00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:04.934
-That's amazing.
-It's amazing.

00:04:04.934 --> 00:04:07.767
And she is the most brilliant,
talented person.

00:04:07.767 --> 00:04:09.901
She's not just a director
and a writer,

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:11.133
but she's also an artist.

00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:14.567
She's a painter,
and this is, the movie,

00:04:14.567 --> 00:04:18.100
the screenplay is based
on a short story that she wrote

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.801
and then adapted herself
and then transformed.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:21.467
It's all set
in the world of opera,

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:23.868
but it's also set
in the world of tugboats.

00:04:23.868 --> 00:04:25.634
-That's --
-Which I know we've all

00:04:25.634 --> 00:04:26.868
seen that movie a million times.
-It's such a bummer

00:04:26.868 --> 00:04:28.434
because I have that idea
and I'm now like, huh.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:29.734
-Did you?

00:04:29.734 --> 00:04:31.868
I'm like, do it, do it before
someone beats you to it.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:34.100
-It's that thing
where I love --

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:37.234
I love going to -- I love --
I love all kinds of movies.

00:04:37.234 --> 00:04:39.167
You know, I'm not just like --
but what I think --

00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:41.767
when I think that
we're at our best as an industry

00:04:41.767 --> 00:04:43.133
and when I think
we're as our best,

00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:45.734
like as an audience is
when we have different options.

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:47.467
And I'm just really proud to be,
I don't know,

00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:50.601
part of an independent offering
that's totally original,

00:04:50.601 --> 00:04:52.300
that's a little bit
like a French movie,

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.901
but without subtitles
and in English.

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:55.367
-That's very nice.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:57.100
-Every time I watch
a French movie, I'm like,

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:58.234
"Couldn't you done it easy?

00:04:58.234 --> 00:04:59.767
Couldn't you made it
easier for me?"

00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:01.234
-For me, for me.

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.133
-Now I can't even look
at my phone during this movie.

00:05:03.133 --> 00:05:05.067
-How rude.
-You --

00:05:05.067 --> 00:05:08.367
and you auditioned
for Rebecca Miller years ago.

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:10.300
-I was a literal teenager.
-Wow.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.868
And I auditioned for --
I auditioned

00:05:11.868 --> 00:05:13.968
for her a week before I met you.

00:05:13.968 --> 00:05:15.567
No, I auditioned --

00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:16.801
-I remember you were
always like you

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:17.901
I'm so excited.

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:19.334
I just auditioned
for Rebecca Miller.

00:05:19.334 --> 00:05:21.133
-I was 19 years old,

00:05:21.133 --> 00:05:23.167
and she was making
an incredible movie

00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:24.634
called "The Ballad
of Jack and Rose."

00:05:24.634 --> 00:05:26.300
And I was going to Vassar
at the time,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.501
and I was taking
a romantic poetry course.

00:05:28.501 --> 00:05:30.634
And I remember going into
the audition,

00:05:30.634 --> 00:05:32.734
and you know how it is.
You're like one fish

00:05:32.734 --> 00:05:35.634
in a sea of an ocean
of actresses.

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:37.601
And I didn't know if I was
going to get the part,

00:05:37.601 --> 00:05:39.167
but I just wanted to let
her know what the screenplay

00:05:39.167 --> 00:05:41.534
had meant to me because
I really love her writing.

00:05:41.534 --> 00:05:45.234
And I just handed her a poem
that reminded me

00:05:45.234 --> 00:05:48.000
of the screenplay
that she'd written.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.434
And so when I met her,
she was actually my last meeting

00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:52.834
before the pandemic kicked in.
-Oh, wow.

00:05:52.834 --> 00:05:54.467
-And I walked in and she goes,
"It's really nice to meet you."

00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:56.501
And I said,
"Well, technically we've met."

00:05:56.501 --> 00:05:59.367
And I remind her,
and she didn't remember me.

00:05:59.367 --> 00:06:00.667
Hopefully my acting
has improved since then,

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:02.534
but -- but she did remember
the poem.

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:06.133
-Oh, and that's very sweet
to have a poem remembered.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:07.267
She's like,
In all my years,

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:09.200
no one had
the nerve to hand me a poem.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.167
-The nerve -- the nerve
or the nerd?

00:06:12.167 --> 00:06:13.567
-It's a little, I think,
it's a combo of both

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:15.334
when you give someone a poem.
-A nervy nerd.

00:06:15.334 --> 00:06:16.534
-[ Laughs ]
-That seems lovely.

00:06:16.534 --> 00:06:18.634
-This is -- you're also a
producer on this film.

00:06:18.634 --> 00:06:21.000
-I am.
-And I'm wondering, like,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.100
how, you know,
because again,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:24.968
you're this wonderful actor.

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:26.667
And then do you also
have to bring that in?

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:27.701
And this is what I also love.

00:06:27.701 --> 00:06:29.033
This is such a great
New York cast.

00:06:29.033 --> 00:06:30.601
Marisa Tomei and Peter Dinklage.

00:06:30.601 --> 00:06:32.767
This is a real East Coast
posse right here.

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:35.067
And do you ever
as a producer go,

00:06:35.067 --> 00:06:37.567
hey, maybe a little bit
more like this or do you...?

00:06:37.567 --> 00:06:39.234
-What do you mean?
-Like, do you ever give

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:41.300
your other actors like,
hey, I just want to remind you

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.634
that I'm also, you know?
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.167
-Yeah, good. Yeah.
-I have to.

00:06:44.167 --> 00:06:45.968
-You got to, right?
You care about the product.

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:48.033
Your name's out there.
-I just don't know that

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:50.167
it's been a complete experience
since I've crushed the spirit

00:06:50.167 --> 00:06:51.601
of the person that I'm with.

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:53.200
You know, that's just kind of --
What?

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.367
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.367 --> 00:06:57.434
I am kidding.
I promise.

00:06:57.434 --> 00:06:59.167
-You should give
all your notes from here

00:06:59.167 --> 00:07:01.200
on out via poem
and just be like,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.567
I have something to say
about your acting,

00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:04.501
but I would like you
to read a poem.

00:07:04.501 --> 00:07:06.968
-Okay. So if I give --
I'm going to try this.

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:08.834
-Okay, good.

00:07:08.834 --> 00:07:10.834
-Or I'm going to deny this.
-Oh!

00:07:10.834 --> 00:07:12.667
-I don't know
if I can fly this,

00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:14.634
but I'm going to try this.

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.267
-This is like a magic trick,
and I don't like it.

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:18.033
[ Laughter ]

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:19.634
I don't like how quickly
you're rhyming.

00:07:19.634 --> 00:07:21.667
[ Laughs ]

00:07:21.667 --> 00:07:23.701
-If you don't like it --
No, I'm just gonna stop.

00:07:23.701 --> 00:07:25.267
-Okay, good.
I have more questions

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:26.801
to ask you, and we're going
to show a clip of the movie.

00:07:26.801 --> 00:07:28.200
Right back with more
from Anne Hathaway.

